The project produces two primary output files:

- `test_predictions.csv` — contains actual and predicted sales for the test period.
- `future_sales_forecast.csv` — contains the predicted sales for January to June 2026.

Visualizations can be saved as image files and included in the project repository.

## 8. MODEL EVALUATION METRICS

### 8.1 Mean Absolute Error (MAE)

MAE measures the average absolute difference between actual and predicted values.

MAE = (1/n) × Σ |Actual − Predicted|

A lower MAE indicates that predictions are closer to the actual observations on average.

### 8.2 Root Mean Squared Error (RMSE)

RMSE measures the square root of the average squared prediction error.

RMSE = √[(1/n) × Σ(Actual − Predicted)²]

RMSE gives greater weight to larger errors than MAE.

### 8.3 Coefficient of Determination (R²)

R² measures how well the model explains the variation in the target variable relative to a baseline that predicts the mean of the observed target values.

An R² value closer to 1 generally indicates a better fit on the evaluated dataset. However, it does not guarantee that future forecasts will be equally accurate.

## 9. RESULTS AND DISCUSSION

The trained Linear Regression model was evaluated using the last 10 observations of the historical dataset.

### 9.1 Model performance

| Evaluation Metric | Result |
|---|---:|
| Mean Absolute Error (MAE) | 5.07 |
| Root Mean Squared Error (RMSE) | 6.41 |
| R² Score | 0.836 |

The MAE of approximately 5.07 indicates that the average absolute prediction error is around 5.07 sales units, assuming the target values are measured in sales units.

The RMSE of approximately 6.41 indicates the magnitude of prediction errors while penalizing larger errors more strongly.

The R² score of approximately 0.836 indicates that the model explains about 83.6% of the variation in the test observations relative to the mean-based baseline.

These results suggest that the model captures a substantial portion of the observed sales pattern in the test period. However, its performance on future data may differ.

